---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CURRENT, STATUS, type Status } from '../lib/products';

// In the order it lands, and where each piece stands. The statuses come from
// the Gateway's ROADMAP, "UniFi", and nowhere else.
const order: [string, Status, string][] = [
  ['Network, read-only', 'released', 'Every adopted device, switch port, PoE draw and WAN link, and which WAN is carrying traffic, as tags that sync themselves. A switch adopted tomorrow shows up by itself, and one that disappears goes bad quality instead of vanishing out from under its alarms.'],
  ['Clients and OT watch', 'released', 'Clients keyed by MAC, never IP, because the controller shows addresses a machine left hours ago. Mark a network as OT, accept what is on it, and anything new raises an alarm. Link a PLC to its switch port once, and when it goes quiet it tells you which port and whether the link is up. Proven against a lab controller and read on two real consoles. A real cable pull and a phone joining SCADA on purpose still have to happen on real gear, and until they do, those two alarms are proven in the lab and nowhere else.'],
  ['Screens and the topology map', 'planned', 'An overview with a WAN strip that goes amber when the site is running on the backup link, and a topology map laid out the same way every time, so nothing jiggles on a wall board. Click a switch, see which lines on the Plant tree it feeds.'],
  ['Events', 'planned', "The controller's events websocket, so a switch dropping off shows up in seconds and lands in the logbook. Polling stays the truth: a dead websocket costs speed, never correctness."],
  ['Network writes', 'planned', 'Locate LED first, because it is zero risk and proves the whole write path on real gear. Then PoE mode, port power-cycle, WLAN on and off, client block, and shutting the port an unknown device just showed up on. Every one through the write gate.'],
  ['Protect, Access and PDUs', 'planned', 'Cameras as entities with detections as triggers and a snapshot on the alarm that pages you. Door events in the logbook and unlock behind the gate. Outlet power feeding the energy numbers. Each one merges after it has run on real gear, not before.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="UniFi in HotLoop: read-only today, Network first"
  description="A native UniFi integration for HotLoop, written in Go. The read-only Network driver shipped in 4.17.0. Screens, writes, Protect, Access and PDUs come next."
  ogAlt="UniFi in HotLoop, read-only in 4.17.0."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">UniFi</span><span class="chip ok">Read-only in {CURRENT}</span></div>
      <h1>Your UniFi network, next to the machines it feeds.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        When a PLC goes quiet, the first question is always the same: is it the PLC or the wire? Today you answer it
        by walking to the UniFi app, or to the rack. HotLoop has a native UniFi integration, built in Go from
        scratch, so the answer sits on the same screen as the machine: which switch port it's on, whether that port
        is up, and what it's drawing.
      </p>
      <div class="notice">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>The read-only half shipped in {CURRENT}. Nothing writes to a console yet.</strong>
          <p>
            The Network driver and the clients work are in the Gateway today, and the Edge Relay can poll a console
            too. Writes come only after the write path has been proven on a lab controller and one empty port, so
            nothing in this release can cycle a port or change a setting on your network. It lives in the base of the
            lineup, so <a href="/products/">every HotLoop product</a> gets it. The setup, down to pinning the
            console's certificate, is in <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/unifi/">the docs</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Already paid for itself</span>
        <h2>It got fooled by a dead backup link, once.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          On 2026-09-28 the read-only driver went onto the house Cloud Gateway and an Express 7 for the first time,
          and it said the 5G backup WAN was up. It wasn't. It had been unplugged for two days. The console's WAN
          block still said up, with a stale 80% signal, and that's the field HotLoop read. Only the console's own
          health checks knew the truth, and nothing had needed the backup yet, so nobody else knew either.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          So the driver reads the health checks now, a dead backup reads down, and a test holds it to those exact
          fields. That's the whole reason this integration exists: you find out on a Monday afternoon, not the night
          the fiber gets cut.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">The order</span>
        <h2>Network first, then everything that hangs off it.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="order">
        {order.map(([name, status, what]) => (
          <li class="card reveal">
            <div class="cluster head"><h3>{name}</h3><span class={`chip ${STATUS[status].chip}`}>{STATUS[status].label}</span></div>
            <p>{what}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">How it works</span>
        <h2>A protocol driver, not a side system.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A UniFi console is a device in HotLoop, same as a PLC, and its switches, ports and WAN links are tags. That
          one decision means alarms, trends, entities, rules, the audit, OPC UA and MCP all work on your network the
          day the driver does, without building half of each again for one vendor.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>One login, not forty</h3><p>One session per console, reused for every read, with a budget on how often it logs in. Logging in on every check got our own Cloud Gateway to lock us out, twice. HotLoop waits a lockout out instead of making it longer.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A pinned certificate</h3><p>The first connect fails on purpose and shows you the console's key fingerprint. Check it, paste it in. From then on a console with a different key is refused during the handshake, before your password goes anywhere.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>WAN failover</h3><p>Which WAN is carrying traffic, as a tag you can alarm on. Know the plant failed over to the backup link before the data bill tells you.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Protected ports, when writes land</h3><p>HotLoop won't cycle an uplink, or the port powering the backup WAN, no matter who asks. Worked out from the topology, not typed in by hand, so nobody forgets one.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A cooldown on power-cycles</h3><p>Fifteen minutes per port. A rule that cycles a PLC every two minutes is how you cook a power supply, and one that needs more than one cycle has a problem a cycle won't fix.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>What it will never do</h3><p>No VLANs, firewall rules, DHCP, static IPs, SSIDs or firmware. We've watched a switch lose its controller over one static IP change. That's the UniFi app's job. HotLoop does the few things an operator needs at 2am.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="prose muted after">
        Console passwords are entered in HotLoop and stored encrypted, with the key kept outside the database, so a
        dump or a backup doesn't hand anybody the keys to your network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="muted small">
        UniFi is a trademark of Ubiquiti Inc. HotLoop is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or a partner of Ubiquiti.
        This is an independent integration that talks to your own console, on your own network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  ol.order { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); }
  ol.order li { padding: var(--hl-s5) var(--hl-s6); }
  ol.order .head { justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: var(--hl-s2); }
  ol.order h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin: 0; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }
  .small { font-size: var(--hl-fs-xs); }
</style>
